{"id": "SATM2ULXWWS", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A line perpendicular to $5x - 2y = 8$ passes through the point $(1, 11)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line? (Enter only the $y$-coordinate.)", "options": [], "correct_answer": "57/5", "explanation": "Rewriting $5x - 2y = 8$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = \\frac{5}{2}x - 4$, so the slope is $\\frac{5}{2}$. A perpendicular line has slope $-\\frac{2}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(1, 11)$: $y - 11 = -\\frac{2}{5}(x - 1)$, which gives $y = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{2}{5} + 11 = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{57}{5}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\frac{57}{5}$."}
{"id": "SATM2UOEUKX", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $\\ell$ is perpendicular to the line $5x - 3y = 9$ and passes through the point $(6, -1)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $\\ell$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "13/5", "explanation": "Rewrite $5x - 3y = 9$ in slope-intercept form: $y = \\frac{5}{3}x - 3$, so the slope is $\\frac{5}{3}$. The slope of a perpendicular line is the negative reciprocal: $-\\frac{3}{5}$. Use point-slope form with $(6, -1)$: $y - (-1) = -\\frac{3}{5}(x - 6)$. Simplify: $y + 1 = -\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{18}{5}$. Then $y = -\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{18}{5} - 1 = -\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{13}{5}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\frac{13}{5}$. A common error is using the same slope $\\frac{5}{3}$ instead of the negative reciprocal, which gives a $y$-intercept of $-1 - \\frac{5}{3}(6) = -11$ (concept gap about perpendicular slopes). Another error is computing the negative reciprocal as $\\frac{3}{5}$ (positive) instead of $-\\frac{3}{5}$, yielding a $y$-intercept of $-1 - \\frac{18}{5} = -\\frac{23}{5}$ (sign error)."}
