{"id": "SATM2V5CYWD", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The equation $3x + 5y = 45$ represents a relationship between $x$ and $y$. What is the value of $y$ when $x = 0$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$9$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$15$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$45$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-9$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $9$. Substituting $x = 0$ into $3(0) + 5y = 45$ gives $5y = 45$. Dividing both sides by $5$ yields $y = 9$. The value $15$ comes from dividing $45$ by $3$ instead of $5$, confusing the coefficients. The value $45$ results from ignoring the coefficient of $y$ entirely. The value $-9$ is a sign error applied when dividing."}
{"id": "SATM2V5YNXZ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The equation $5x - 2y = 20$ represents a line in the $xy$-plane. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-10$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-4$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$4$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$10$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $-10$. The $y$-intercept occurs when $x = 0$. Substituting $x = 0$ into $5(0) - 2y = 20$ gives $-2y = 20$, so $y = -10$. $-4$ results from dividing $20$ by $5$ to find the $x$-intercept. $4$ comes from a sign error when dividing by $-2$. $10$ is obtained by ignoring the negative sign on the $2y$ term."}
{"id": "SATM2V6YSRQ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $7x + 3y = 41$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(5, -2)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-\\frac{29}{7}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{29}{7}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{1}{7}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-\\frac{41}{3}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewrite line $p$ in slope-intercept form: $3y = -7x + 41$, so $y = -\\frac{7}{3}x + \\frac{41}{3}$. The slope of line $p$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}$, so the perpendicular slope is $\\frac{3}{7}$. Using point-slope form with $(5, -2)$: $y + 2 = \\frac{3}{7}(x - 5)$. Simplifying: $y = \\frac{3}{7}x - \\frac{15}{7} - \\frac{14}{7} = \\frac{3}{7}x - \\frac{29}{7}$. The $y$-intercept is $-\\frac{29}{7}$. The value $\\frac{29}{7}$ results from a sign error when combining $-\\frac{15}{7}$ and $-\\frac{14}{7}$. The value $\\frac{1}{7}$ comes from using $-\\frac{3}{7}$ as the perpendicular slope instead of $\\frac{3}{7}$, which gives $y = -\\frac{3}{7}x + \\frac{15}{7} - \\frac{14}{7} = -\\frac{3}{7}x + \\frac{1}{7}$. The value $-\\frac{41}{3}$ results from incorrectly using $\\frac{7}{3}$ as the perpendicular slope."}
